## Harnessing the biocatalytic attributes and applied perspectives of nanoengineered laccases―A review
Abstract
In the recent past, numerous new types of nanostructured carriers, as support matrices, have been engineered to advance the traditional enzyme immobilization strategies. The current research aimed to develop a robust enzyme-based biocatalytic platform and its effective deployment in the industrial biotechnology sectors at large and catalysis area, in particular, as low-cost biocatalytic systems. Suitable coordination between the target enzyme molecules and surface pendent multifunctional entities of nanostructured carriers has led an effective and significant contribution in myriad novel industrial, biotechnological, and biomedical applications. As compared to the immobilization on planar two-dimensional (2-D) surface, the unique physicochemical, structural and functional attributes of nano-engineered matrices, such as high surface-to-volume ratio, surface area, robust chemical and mechanical stability, surface pendant functional groups, outstanding optical, thermal, and electrical characteristics, resulted in the concentration of the immobilized entity being substantially higher, which is highly requisite from applied bio-catalysis perspective. Besides inherited features, nanostructured materials-based enzyme immobilization aided additional features, such as (1) ease in the preparation or green synthesis route, (2) no or minimal use of surfactants and harsh reagents, (3) homogeneous and well-defined core-shell nanostructures with thick enzyme shell, and (4) nano-size can be conveniently tailored within utility limits, as compared to the conventional enzyme immobilization. Moreover, the growing catalytic needs can be fulfilled by multi-enzymes coimmobilization on these nanostructured materials-based support matrices. This review spotlights the unique structural and functional attributes of several nanostructured materials, including carbon nanotubes, graphene, and its derivate constructs, nanoparticles, nanoflowers, and metal-organic frameworks as robust matrices for laccase immobilization. The later half of the review focuses on the applied perspective of immobilized laccases for the degradation of emergent contaminants, biosensing cues, and lignin deconstruction and high-value products.
Keywords: Nanostructured carriers; Enzyme immobilization; Laccase; Biocatalytic platform; Multifunctional entities; Applications
## 1. Introduction
In recent years, nanostructured carrier development, though using different materials, has gained special research interests in applied biotechnology at large and enzyme biocatalysis, in particular. Materials-based nano-engineered carriers offer multifunctional features that could support enzyme immobilization for their potential applications in biotechnology, environmental engineering, and biosensing areas. In addition, the immobilization of enzymes with industrial interest using nanoengineering support matrices facilitate the highly requisite features, i.e., ease in handling, convenience in catalyst separation after the reaction, multiple reusabilities, induce pH and thermal stability, higher stability under harsh reach conditions, and overall high cost-effective ratio with low product cost (Sheldon and van Pelt, 2013; Zdarta et al., 2018; Bilal et al., 2018; Bilal and Iqbal, 2019; Bilal et al., 2019). Besides the features mentioned above, enzyme immobilization also requires inertness, i.e., the support matrices should not interfere with the catalytic attribute of the enzyme, which thereby could compromise its catalytic activity (Mitchell et al., $2\cap\mathop{\mathcal{T}}_{s_{4}}^{\sim}\:)$ .
Recent studies have been widely practiced the enzyme immobilization techniques combined with numerous $\mathrm{\hat{\su}_{\mathrm{\DeltaF}}}$ port matrices engineered at nanoscale level (Barbosa et al., 2015; dos Santos et al., 2015; Bilal et al., 2017). Even so, following enzyme immobilization into/onto suitable support material, the deployment of immobilizedenzyme could lead to an increase in the overall catalytic performance owing to the enzyme-carrier interaction (Verma et al., 2012). In addition, the optimally immobilizedenzyme induce and reserve the hyper-activity and enzyme stability, even under different reaction environment without altering the enzyme microenvironment (Rodrigues et al., 2013; Bilal and Iqbal, 2019; Bilal et al., 2019). Subsequently, it is highly critical to scrutinize the enzyme carriers with robust, biocompatible, and recoverable features (Temoçin et al., 2018). Numerous industrially requisite attributes, including effective enzyme loading, surface-to-volume ratio, high surface area, and noticeable biocatalytic yield render nano-engineered material matrices as ideal candidates for commercial-scale bio-catalysis in aqueous/non-aqueous environment (Doğaç et al., 2017; Adeel et al., 2018; Bilal et al., 2018; Bilal and Iqbal, 2019; Bilal et al., 2019). The nanoengineered material as support matrices demonstrates extraordinary advantages in terms of reaction performance and permits the construction of a nanocarrier–enzyme association microenvironment for optimal reaction processes. Manifold recovering and reutilizing capacities accompanied by easy separation can substantially trimdown/lower the overall cost of enzyme-based bioprocesses in industrial settings (Wong et al., 2017; Huang et al., 2018; Bilal and Iqbal, 2019). So far, an array of multifunctionalengineered nanocarriers, such as carbon nanotubes, graphene, and its derivatives nanoparticles, nanoflowers, and metal-organic frameworks as robust matrices have been developed and used for robust bio-catalysis. Scheme 1 shows the exploitation of various nanostructured materials for enzyme immobilization and their effective deployment for different applications.
During the past several years, the enzyme immobilization strategies vary as per the structural, functional, and physicochemical attributes of both participating counterparts, i.e., target enzyme and nano-engineered materials-based support matrices. This also significantly relies on the interfacial interactions, involved during the immobilization processes between the enzyme and carriers, subject to their respective applications in the industrial settings (Bilal and Iqbal, 2019). Following careful consideration of the above-mentioned points, herein, we reviewed the recent developments in engineering unique nano-structured carriers and their effective deployment for laccase immobilization purposes. The crux of the work focusses on the exceptional structural and functional features of numerous nanostructured materials, including carbon nanotubes, graphene, and its derivate constructs, metallic and magnetic nanoparticles, nanoflowers, and metal-organic frameworks as robust matrices for laccase immobilization. Several applied perspectives such as the degradation of emergent contaminants using immobilized laccases, immobilized-laccases based biosensing cues, and lignin deconstruction and high-value product generation via highly efficient laccase-assisted catalysis, are also discussed with suitable examples in the later half of the review.
## 2. Carbon nanotubes as supports for laccase immobilization
In recent years, carbon nanotubes (CNTs) have been identified as nanostructured materials with potential capability to construct novel materials for a large number of applications such as nanocatalysis, treatment of water and air, biosensors fuel cells, capacitors, and energy storage ad conversion (Endo et al., 2006; Park et al., 2012). High adsorption capacity, huge surface area, and superb conductivity that promote the electrons transfer between the surface of the electrode and electro-activated species are among the notable attributes that render these materials for broad-spectrum applications (Oliveira et al., 2013; Vargas-Garcıa et al., 2014; Silva et al., 2014). The CNTs are comprised of closed and concentric graphene sheets, which are rolled around the other structures of different diam $\alpha^{2}\in\Gamma\upalpha,$ usually 2 to $30\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The immobilization via covalent or non-covalent attachments of laccases onto the CNTs has been described. Among these, the noncovalent attachment of enzymes is less vulnerable to induce any unfavorable alterations and preserves the intrinsic attributes of both enzymes and CNTs (Asuri et al., 2006). Hence, enzymes have been widely immobilized on CNTs via physical adsorption, but the enzyme activity could be steadily lost by this method (Karajanagi et al., 2004). In contrast, covalent conjugation between enzymes and CNTs is known to furnish durable attachment, improved stability, selectivity, and avoids the enzyme leaching, but the three-dimensional conformation of the enzyme may be damaged (Jiang et al., 2004). The process of surface functionalization can further improve the efficiency of CNTs as support material. Surface functionalization implicates grafting of desirable functional moieties on the surface to achieve CNTs with improved properties. It can affect the dispersibility and interactions with enzymes, thus eloquently modifying the catalytic traits of the immobilized enzyme (Verma et al., 2013). Regardless of which method is employed, the major challenge is to create a strong attachment of enzymes by maintaining their catalytic activities as exhibited by their original form. Particularly, the catalytic features of CNT-immobilized laccases are influenced by a combination of the immobilization method employed and the nanotube interactions (Bilal et al., 2020).
Electrospun nanofibrous membranes offer suitable biocompatible surfaces for the attachment of different enzymes, but after immobilization, the coupled biocatalysts lose a major portion of their original activities presumably due to non-biospecific interactions, apparent alterations in the enzyme diffusion rate, and differences in the enzyme microenvironment. Furthermore, most of the enzymes involve electron transfer during catalytic reactions. Owing to the excellent mechanical strength, high electrical conductivity, elevated thermal resistance, and unique geometrical architecture, MWCNTs have been employed as promisi $\mathrm{\Upsilon^{\circ}g}$ reinforcing materials to modifying the biocatalytic performance of laccase-coupled electrospun fibrous membranes (LCEFMs) (Singh et al., 2012). Reports have evidenced that the introduction of MWNTs could efficiently amplify the direct exchange of electron between biomolecules/proteins and electrodes (Zhao et al., 2006; Lopez et al., 2014). The MWCNTs functionalized LCEFMs (MWCNTs-LCEFMs) were synthesized with laccase by encapsulating MWCNTs inside these nanofibers. The as-synthesized MWCNTs-LCEFMs displayed high activity recovery as well as better operational and storage stability compared to LCEFMs, which are ascribed to increased electron transference in the laccase-driven catalytic reaction. In addition to high tensile strength and specific surface area, the resultant MWCNTsLCEFMs also exhibited improved endurances to $\mathrm{\pH}$ and temperature conditions than those of LCEFMs (Dai et al., 2016a). Novel bionanocomposites were synthesized by the covalent immobilization of laccase enzyme onto nano zeolite-CNT composites. Varying quantities of CNTs such as 30, 50, and $70~\mathrm{mg}$ were incorporated to fabricate CNTszeolite nanocomposites. The as-engineered bionanocomposites were silanized, activated by GLU, and used for the immobilization of laccase. The nanobiocatalysts maintained over $90\%$ of its primary catalytic activity after five consecutive reaction cycles. It showed significantly higher stability retaining more than $80\%$ of its activity at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . The free form of enzyme dropped more than $60\%$ of its activity after 8 days of storage, whereas the nano zeolite-CNT composites immobilized was capable of retaining about $80\%$ of its highest activity (Mahmoodi et al., 2020).
In a report, Xu et al. (2013) revealed chitosan and polyvinyl alcohol (PVA/CS) biocomposite nanofibrous membrane a superior carrier for laccase immobilization over traditional support materials due to a huge surface area, high porosity, and excellent permeability. In order to further improve the enzyme loading capacity of the carrier, reduce the diffusion resistance, increase the electrical conductivity, and furnishing a biocompatible microenvironment for laccase immobilization, they introduced MWNTs into the earlier synthesized PVA/CS membrane. The idea was based on the speculation that MWNTs inclusion could provide efficient networks for electron transference and thereby accelerating the electron transfer rate of redox reactions during the catalysis (Xu et al., 2015). For this, the laccase was covalently immobilized on the surface of a new composite nanofibrous membrane (100-200 nm) composed of polyvinyl alcohol/chitosan/MWNTs (Figure 1). The MWNTs-incorporated nanofibrous membranes result in substantially high activity retention and enzyme loading of the immobilized enzyme (907 mg enzyme/g support) compared with those lacking MWNTs incorporated nanofibrous composite (862 mg enzyme/g support). Immobilized nanobiocatalytic system presented elevated stability, and promising repeated use capacity. Characterization by cyclic voltammetry revealed the augmented electrochemical properties of the nanofibrous composite by the inclusion of MWNTs. Thus, it can be inferred that CNTs might exert a noteworthy contribution to improving the activity of nanofibers immobilized laccase by enhancing electron exchange between the substrate molecules and biocatalyst. Covalently attachment of laccase from Myceliophthora thermophila onto modified MWNT coated over supporting membrane results in the generation of a permeable bio-barrier, which can be employed in continuous or multi-batch bioprocesses. Various polymeric matrices including agarose, cellulose nitrate, and polyvinyl alcohol were initially appraised as supports for MWNT. Among these, cellulose nitrate supported with an additional amount of $0.21\mathrm{mg}$ MWNT furnished the maximum enzyme loading $(0.286~\mathrm{U/mg)}$ . The resultant nanobiocatalyst exhibited remarkable functional stability retaining over $\mathbf{\Omega}^{\mathrm{co}}\mathrm{~}$ of its original catalytic performance after 10 successive reaction cycles. It also showed enhanced stability against varying $\mathrm{\pH},$ temperature, and acetone conditions, where the immobilized laccase retained about $50\%$ activity by incubation $\therefore\cdot0\%$ of acetone $\left(\mathrm{v/v}\right)$ for $6\mathrm{h},$ while only $21\%$ was maintained by the free counterpart under these conditions (Othman et al., 2016).
3. Graphene, graphene oxide and reduced graphene oxide as supports for laccase immobilization
Graphene is an emerging kind two-dimensional carbon nanostructure that has an array of applications in the electrochemical filed due to its outstanding electrical conductivity, distinctive sheet structu $\cos\alpha-1$ robust specific surface area (Ma et al., 2019; Zhu et al., 2019; Zhang et al., $2\mathrm{c}^{1\mathrm{q}})$ . Due to these features, graphene materials are conceived as suitable carriers for enzyme immobilization, and many research groups have reported the effective immobilization of laccases on these materials. For example, Dong et al. (2019) assembled a laccase-graphene nanocomposite by the interactions of pristine fewlayer graphene (FLG) with laccase enzyme. Characterization analysis by adsorption isotherm, FTIR, and AFM revealed the high loading of laccase on this FLG. Based on measuring the interactive forces and types of binding between graphene and laccase, the high enzyme loading on FLG was speculated to the larger surface area of FLG and non-covalent p-p stacking. The synthesized nanocomposite exhibited superior resistance to varying $\mathrm{\pH},$ , temperature, and storage conditions than that to the free form of laccase. However, it displays obvious drawbacks in the role of van der Waals force between the sheets, and the resultant aggregation markedly influences the fabrication and application of graphene-based materials (Deng et al., 2018; Zhao et al., 2019). The use of pristine graphene is restricted for practical exploitation due to the lack of surface functionalities, which create covalent linkages with biomolecules, enzymes, and polymers. In this connection, researchers have rekindled their interest to implicate functionalized graphene, like graphene oxide (GO) and reduced GO for immobilizing laccase enzyme (Adeel et al., 2018).
In the last several years, various methods such as micromechanical exfoliation, chemical vapour deposition, and chemical synthesis from graphite have been proposed to produce graphene (Wang et al., 2011). Chemical synthesis of graphene from graphite is among the most elegant method for graphene synthesis with notable advantages of high-volume production, easy chemical modification, and scalability. Nevertheless, some defects could be introduced in graphene during the oxidation or reduction steps resulting in structural damage of the graphene. Hence, chemically produced graphene is also referred to as chemically modified graphene in some reports (Park and Ruoff, 2009). In brief, graphene production by chemical method implicates initial oxidation of graphite-to-graphite oxide, followed by thermal or mechanical exfoliation of graphite oxide to GO sheets. The as-synthesized GO sheets are hydrophilic material that carries oxygen functional groups on their basal planes and edges (Wang et al., 2011).
Graphene oxide (GO)-based nanostructures can potentially accelerate the transfer of electrons between the catalytic support and enzyme. These nanomaterials provide excellent biocompatibility, stability, good functionalization ability, and high water dispersibility due to the presence of oxygen-containing functional moieties (Li et al., 2017; Samak et al., 2018). Its easy preparation and several active functionalities such as epoxy, hydroxyl, and carboxyl groups are the noteworthy assets that are involved in a large number of different chemical reactions. They also result in the establishment of robust support-enzyme interactions and thus biocatalytic performance. All these characteristics render GO as a suitable support for the immobilization of laccases (Mitchell and Pérez-Ramírez, 2011). The kind of interactions between graphene support and enzyme exhibit a crucial impact on enzyme loading, and catalytic performance and stability of the enzyme. Moreover, the toxic effects of GO are increased with the degree of oxidation and extent of functional moieties, which indicates the environmentfriendliness of pristine graphene compared with other graphene-family nanomaterials (Li et al., 2016). Different functionalization approaches can be used for surface modification of GO to expand its application in different technologies, such as enzyme immobilization, polymer grafting, composite materials, and as the building blocks for high-performance nanostructured thin films (Adeel et al., 2018). The incorporation of oxygen-containing functional groups increases the chemical reactivity of GO compared to pristine graphene making GO as a promising candidate for additional modifications. The first modification of GO was carried out by the activation of the carboxylic group using thionyl chloride via amide formation followed by the coupling of octadecylamine (Niyogi et al., 2006). Likewise, the epoxy group was also activated by ring-opening reactions. This type of surface modification tends to dissolve GO in water and many organic solvents due to activation of the hydrophilic nature of GO and displays notable swelling and intercalation properties (Fang et al., 2009). The non-covalent modification of GO is categorized into four directions such as pi-pi stacking, hydrogen bonding, cation-pi, and van der Walls interactions. Similarly, the covalent-functionalization involves three categories: ring-opening reaction of the epoxy group on the basal plane generating amine groups, cycloaddition reactions of rGO, and the nucleophilic reaction of carboxylic groups at the edges of the sheet by an amine or hydroxyl groups (Yang et al., 2008; Lu et al., 2009a). In general, non-covalent interactions are applied to immobilize enzymes on non-modified graphene due to the lack of surface functionalities, which causes a little alteration in the enzyme conformation (Georgakilas et al., 2016). In a comparative immobilization study, Zhang and coworkers, (2012) found that the rGO displays a greater capacity for loading, activity, and catalytic stability of enzyme compared to GO because of high reduction magnitudes on its surface.
On GO, the laccase enzyme can be immobilized by cross-linking, physical adsorption, carrier binding, and covalent linkages (Chen et al., 2017). Several researchers have assessed GO-based materials for immobilizing laccases. Recently, Mahmoodi and Saffar-Dastgerdi, (2020) carried out the covalent immobilization of laccase enzyme onto zeolite-GO nanocomposites to synthesize new nanobiocatalytic systems for environmental purposes. Zeolites possess various structures and reactive sites that provide enzyme-support interaction. They have shown great promise as a suitable carrier to constitute a stable and high-performance enzyme system owing to marked surface area, mechanical and thermal stability, and tunable hydrophilic/hydrophobic properties (Soy et al., 2010; Mitchell and Pérez-Ramírez, 2011). Zeolites constitute a large group of silicates that host a huge percentage of water within their impermeable structure. This aqueous microenvironment is beneficial for the enzymes to maintain its active three-dimensional conformational structure (Wehaidy et al., 2019). The GOzeolite composite materials were the silanized and cross-linked by glutaraldehyde (Figure 2). The modified materials were used for laccase immobilization to develop laccase-coupled nanobiocatalysts, which were characterized by different techniques and utilized for contaminant degradation. The as-fabricated novel nanobiocatalysts showed good recycling ability over five consecutive reaction cycles, elevated temperature stability, and high storage stability.
Although CNTs and GO have revealed excellent abilities for enzyme immobilization due to their unique structural and mechanic attributes, which ensure substantial enzyme adsorption capacity. Additionally, carbon-based nanomaterials can enable the direct transfer of an electron from the enzyme’s catalytic region to the support, leading to flexible bio-electrode design for biofuel cell and biosensor without extra electron transfer mediators (Leech et al., 2012). Nevertheless, enzyme molecules are large than that to traditional metal catalysts such as Pd, Pt, Ir, and Ru resulting in low catalytic density per unit area (Yang et al., 2012). Therefore, a multilayer 3D structure is required to augment the catalytic density of immobilized enzymes. GO and CNTs can be coated with an epoxide, carboxyl, and hydroxyl groups to facilitate surface functionalization and metal ion binding for constructing a 3D structure for efficient enzyme immobilization Xu et al., 2010; Chua and Pumera, 2013; $\mathbf{\mu}_{\mathbf{\mu}_{-}}^{\mathbf{\sigma}_{1}}\mathbf{a}_{\mathbf{\mu}_{\mathbf{\sigma}}}\mathbf{\sigma}_{\mathbf{\sigma}}$ et al., 2013). Li et al. (2017) achieved enhanced enzyme activity and catalytic stability by immobilizing a laccase enzyme onto copper phosphate/CNTs/GO hybrid nanocomposite. Besides, the high loading of laccase on this support and good mass transfer efficacy between the immobilized enzyme and substrate was attributed to the incorporation of copper phosphate petals during the formation of the nanostructure.
Covalent bonding of $T_{\mathbf{\delta}}$ . versicolor laccase on functionalized GO-based MNPs displayed an elevated activity of $0.77{\mathrm{u/mg}}$ (Rouhani et al., 2018). Likewise, the covalent coupling of laccase from genetically modified Aspergillus onto GO yielded efficient loading of laccase with a rate of $1565~\mathrm{\mathrm{{ng/g}}}$ (Kashefi et al., 2019). In all of the above-stated investigations, the pH, thermal, storage, and operational stabilities of the nanoimmobilized enzymes were meaningfully improved than that to their laccase counterparts. Nevertheless, the weak cross-linking between –COOH and NH2- markedly influence the immobilization process of laccase. In this connection, Chen and coworkers, (2017) suggested the use of hydrochloride/N-hydroxysuccinimide (EDC/NHS) for the activation of the –COOH groups of GO, and then allowed to associate laccase with amino $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . This practice greatly reinforced the amido linkage leading to increased stability of the immobilized biocatalyst (Chen et al., 2017). Though the nanobiocomposite performed well for decolorization purposes, the enzyme loading was rather low (i.e. $30\mathrm{mg/g)}$ due to competitive cross-linking between amino Fe3O4 and laccase towards the modified GO. Qiao and Liu (2019) attempted to improve the activation method for GO. They used EDC/NHS to design a nanocomposite system by successfully immobilizing CoA laccase from B. subtilis on magnetic GO via covalent interactions. The as-synthesized magnetic support showed a tremendously high binding ability $({\sim}145.04~\mathrm{mg/g)}$ and was capable of maintaining the highest enzyme activities at $\mathrm{pH}~7.0\$ , and $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for a reaction of $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The thermal, $\mathrm{\pH},$ storage and operational stabilities of the immobilized laccase were considerably enhanced than those to the free form of enzymes. It also presented a superior tolerating capacity in the presence of inhibiting agents, organic solvents, salts, and metal ions. The efficient magnetic recovery permitted MGO-coupled to retain over $70\%$ of its original catalytic performance after 10 repeated reaction batches. In a recent study, a modified Hummer's method was employed to synthesize GO, which was then used as a support for immobilizing laccase from genetically modified Aspergillus. Covalent coupling of enzyme onto GO was ensured through the amination of GO by using APTMS, followed by cross-linking with glutaraldehyde as a bifunctional coupling agent (Figure 3). The asengineered nanobiocatalytic system showed promising enzyme loading $(156.5~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and immobilization efficiency of higher than $60\%$ at a corresponding laccase concentration of $0.9m_{>}^{\textrm{c m i}^{-1}}$ (Kashefi et al., 2019). Multipoint covalent attachment of laccase onto fGO has been reported (Patila et al. 2016). The biocatalytic features of the nano assemblies were found to depend on the GO-laccase layers. Notably, the temperature resistance of nanoimmobilized laccase was 4.7-times increased compared to the free state of the enzyme at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
As discussed above, the integration of graphene with nanoparticles might overcome the problem of accumulation, and thus broadening its usefulness in the nanobiocatalysis and electrochemical field. It is described that the supporting material generated by integrating of GO with CuFe2O4 nanoparticles could be easily separated and recovered by using an external magnetic field. In this way, the huge surface area and the presence of functional groups can be preserved by preventing the GO agglomeration with CuFe2O4 nanoparticles. Rouhani et al. (2018) constructed a novel support GO-CuFe2O4- laccase by decorating GO with CuFe2O4 nanoparticles for enhanced covenant immobilization, rapid magnetic recovery, and synergistic efficiency of T. versicolor laccase enzyme due to the occurrence of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ (Figure 4). The resultant GO-CuFe2O4- laccase bioconjugate exhibited better $\mathrm{\pH},$ storage, and thermal tolerance than that to soluble counterpart. It was efficiently applied as a magnetically recoverable and green nanobiocatalytic system to produce arylsulfonyl benzenediols through the aerobic oxidative coupling of sodium benzene sulfonates with benzenediols. Interestingly, it presented a high recovery and reusability profile in 10 consecutive reaction batches with negligible activity loss.
## 4. Silica nanoparticles for laccases immobilization
Mesoporous silica-based materials have gained burgeoning interest as outstanding supports for enzyme attachment due to huge surface area, large pore volume, adjustable pore size, and narrow pore size distribution (Ispas et al., 2009). Over the last two decades, researchers have reported a range of mesoporous silica materials with ordered/disordered pore structures for enzyme immobilization like MCM-41 mesoporous silica, SBA-15 mesoporous silica, and mesoscale cellular foams (Rekuc et al., 2009; Xu et al., $20.09.$ ). Of these materials, the large pore volumes silica mesophases (to encapsulate biomacromolecules) with controlled 3-dimensional architecture are desired for superior enzymatic performance due to the availability of a larger number of reaction sites on the surface of the pores (Park et al., 2005). In addition, accessible internal volume resulting from interconnected pores would be useful for catalysis. Many protocols for the synthesis of mesoporous silica nanoparticles have been reported, where the particle size and pore diameter are controlled by various surfactants and swelling agents. For instance, MCM41 nanoparticles were fabricated a with diameter in the range of $30{-}280~\mathrm{nm}$ ; however, the maximum pore size is restricted to about 6 nm (Lu et al., 2009b). Triblock-copolymer templates, co-solvents, and swelling agents can control larger size SBA-15 nanoparticles in the range of 6 to $50\mathrm{nm}$ , but the smallest SBA-15 particles diameter, reported so far, is approximately $500\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Rejman et al., 2004; Kim et al., 2011). It is a significant challenge to synthesize uniform mesoporous silica nanoparticles of less than $200\mathrm{nm}$ diameter and pore size larger than $10\mathrm{nm}$ by an easily adoptable protocol. To overcome the above-mentioned inadequacies, multipurpose, eco-friendly, and non-surfactant templating approaches are required to synthesize silica nanoparticles with interconnected pores. Tannic acid is a cheap, environmentally friendly, and non-surfactant template that acts as a pore-forming agent, reducing and stabilizing in the synthesis of mesoporous silica nanoparticles. The resulting tannic acid-silica nanoparticles had a surface area, pore-volume, and pore size of $447{\mathrm{m}}^{2}/{\mathrm{g}},0.91{\mathrm{cm}}^{3}/{\mathrm{g}},$ and $10.1\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively (Jiang et al., 2016). Gao and Zharov, (2014) described a one-pot method to produce novel mesoporous silica nanoparticles of $200\ \mathrm{nm}$ diameter with large interconnected mesopores in the range of $6{\cdot}13\ \mathrm{nm}$ using tannic acid as an eco-friendly non-surfactant pore-forming agent. These mesoporous nanoparticles showed high adsorption and protection of the encapsulated enzyme from the environment.
Bebić et al. (2020) focused on the amino functionalization of fumed nanosilica support for its optimal utilization as the ideal support for Myceliophthora thermophila laccase immobilization and elucidated the underlying immobilization mechanism. The maximum specific activity $(13.1~\mathrm{U/mg}$ proteins) and immobilization yield $(68.3\%)$ was recorded at $160~\mathrm{mg/g}$ nanosilica support at optimal $\mathrm{pH}5.0$ . Immobilization of laccase followed simple adsorption and obeyed the pseudo-first-order kinetics. Since the laccase attached to fumed nano-silica particles via the weak Van der Waals, hydrogen, and non-specific electrostatic interactions, the surface of nano-silica support was functionalized with APTMS to introduce amino groups (Figure 5). This procedure led to establishing more durable electrostatic interactions with the nucleophilic groups (carboxyl, thiol, or even hydroxy) of enzymes. Furthermore, fumed nano-silica showed huge surface-to-volume ratio, uniformly distributed particles diameter, biocompatibility, mechanical and thermal steadiness, and high stability towards the organic environment, and microbial contamination (Falahati et al., 2012; Bernal et al., 2014; Banjanac et al., 2016).
Magnetic bio-separation appears an alluring technology for the efficient recovery of mesoporous silica nanocomposites immobilized enzymes by means of a simple exterior magnetic field for repeated processing. Magnetic mesoporous nanomaterials exhibit the distinctive properties of mesoporous materials as well as magnetic features enabling easy separation. Due to these unique attributes, these materials find increasing importance for utilization in enzyme immobilization, environmental mitigation, magnetic resonance imaging, and biomedicine (Ji et al., 2012). Different methods including thermal decomposition, solvothermal, chemical co-precipitation, etc. have been proposed to synthesize MNPs. The integration of mesoporous materials with magnetic nanoparticles is of high interest to develop novel functional materials for catalysis because of the useful magnetic properties of MNPs. The magnetic components in the magnetic mesoporous materials are generally magnetic metals or alloys $(\mathrm{Co},\mathrm{Ni},$ Fe, or PtFe) or oxides such as MFe2O4 $\mathbf{M}=$ Fe, Co, Ni, $Z\mathrm{n},$ etc.) and $\gamma$ -Fe2O3. Due to low toxicity, iron oxide nanoparticles have been extensively utilized to design magnetic composite materials. The magnetically responsive mesoporous composites should have the following features to meet the requirements of practical applications. First, they should be free from being etched in media, which is imperative to retain their magnetic properties. Second, they exhibit enough saturation magnetization for a quick response to the applied magnetic field to ensure efficient magnetic separation. Third, accessible mesopores of the magnetic mesoporous composites enable the use of the high surface area and large pore volume for applications. Besides, superparamagnetism is a highly desired characteristic to preventing the magnetic composite particles from irreversible aggregation and thus ensuring exceptional dispersibility after the elimination of applied magnetic field (Deng et al., 2011). These materials have shown size-selective adsorbing properties for a variety of biomolecules based on size-exclusion mechanism and thus are widely being used in the purification and separation of biomacromolecules from complex reaction systems (Choi et al., 2011). Nevertheless, their applications are restricted to separating peptides, DNA, or only low molecular weight proteins due to the nanosize of the mesopores (Zhang et al., 2011). Hence, magnetic materials with larger mesopores have acquired demand in the field of bio- $=\cos0c.$ ration. To overcome the above-mentioned inadequacies, Wang et al. (2012) fabricated magnetic mesoporous silica nanostructures with various pores size to evaluate the size-specific laccase adsorption and desorption performance of laccase. $\mathsf{T}\boldsymbol{c}_{\mathcal{I}}$ this end, $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}.$ -chelated magnetic mesoporous silica nanoparticles were synthesized by a highly efficient and simple procedure for direct capturing of laccase from the fermented broth of T. versicolor. The as-developed nanocomposites with vary $\L_{\mathcal{V}}$ pore sizes in the range of 3.6 to $27.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ possessed size selectivity for laccase attachment, where the nanoparticles with an average pore size of $14.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ furnished the highest laccase purification (60.6-fold) and enzyme recovery yield $(11.4.7\%)$ . The optimal MMSNPs- ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ conjugate showed a negligible loss in enzyme capturing efficacy up to 10 repeated cycles. Likewise, for the first time, large-pore MMSNPs were engineered using tetraethyl orthosilicate and amine-terminated Jeff amine surfactants as the silica source and template, respectively. The nanoparticle's surface was modified with iminodiacerate via a silane-coupling agent and allowed to chelate with $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ . The resulting magnetic nanoparticles demonstrated elevated adsorption capability and recovered activity for laccase enzyme via metal-affinity adsorption. Thermal tolerance and storage stability of the immobilized nanobiocatalyst was substantially improved. Immobilized enzyme preserved more than $80\%$ of its original activity after 10 consecutive cycles with easy magnetic separation and recovery.
## Magnetic nanoparticles for laccase immobilization
Magnetic nanoparticles (MNPs) are widely employed as support materials for enzyme immobilization since they do not alter the intrinsic biological activities and biocatalytic properties of biomolecules such as enzymes and proteins. Thanks to their tiny architectures, they furnish a larger surface area, which results in elevated loading capacity (Wang et al., 2013). The occurrence of abundant hydroxyl groups on the surface of MNPs enables facile and robust attachment of the enzyme molecules (Bilal et al., 2018). Additionally, the surface of MNPs can be modified with functional moieties for better interaction with target biological entities (Fortes et al., 2017). The use of MNPs eliminates one of the major immobilization challenges, which is the separation of the immobilized bioconjugate from the reaction system after catalytic bioprocesses. Enzyme-immobilized MNPs can be easily removed from the reaction combinations by using an external magnetic field. It is noteworthy that the magnetic behavior considerably shortens process duration, streamlines the reaction termination step, and facilitates the recovery of enzymes for their successive deployment in industrial settings (Ranjbakhsh et al., 2012; Bilal et al., 2018). Ulu et al. (2020) developed Fe3O4-TCS hybrid composites as a novel immobilization matrix for laccase to improve its performance, stability, and recyclability. $\mathrm{r}_{\mathrm{e3}}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ magnetic particles were first synthesized by a coprecipitation route and then functionalized by the thiolated chitosan (Figure 6). The functionalized support was used as a support for laccase attachment. After storage for one month at $4~^{\circ}C$ and $\sim25~^{\circ}C,$ immobilized laccase retained $87\%$ and $80\%$ of its primary activity, respectively; however, the activity was reduced to $73.4\%$ in $3\mathrm{~h~}$ at 55 $^{\circ}{\bf C}.$ After 20 repeated uses, the residual activity of encapsulated enzymes was about $50\%$ of its original activity. Chen et al. (2020) attempted a procedure for laccase of immobilization onto magnetic Fe3O4 nanoparticles (MNPs) with rough surfaces. The MNPs prepared via a solvothermal method were first amino-functionalized APTES, cross-linked with glutaraldehyde, and incubated with T. versicolor laccase to develop
MNPs immobilized laccase (MNP-Lac) (Figure 7). Maximum activity recovery for laccase $(64.7\%)$ was achieved in the presence of glutaraldehyde at a final concentration of $7.5~\mathrm{{mg}\cdot\mathrm{{mL}^{-1}}}$ for $5\mathrm{h}$ . The MNPs-immobilized nanobiocatalyst exhibited enhanced pH tolerance and thermal stability relative to the free enzyme. It retained higher than $80\%$ and $40\%$ of its original activity after 20 days of storage at $4^{\circ}\mathrm C$ and 8 repeated reusability cycles.
Ionic liquids (ILs) have proven as green solvents to preserve enzyme activity, improve steadiness, and effective recycling ability (Zhao, 2016; Itoh, 2017). Modified ILs have been used as connecting agents between organic polymeric and inorganic materials. They also find application to modify the surface functionalities of organic-inorganic nanocomposites for efficient enzyme attachment with augmented stability, biocatalytic activity, and reusability properties (Xiang et al., 2018; Suo et al., 2019). Recently, the laccase enzyme was immobilized onto chitosan-mesoporous hybrid silica SBA-15 nano support using carboxyl-modified ILs as the bridging molecules during the immobilization process (Qiu et al., 2019a). The immobilized enzyme in this way (SBACIL-CS-Lac) showed high immobilization efficiency, activity retention $(75.3\%$ ), $\mathrm{\pH},$ and storage stability. The loading of laccase on SBA-CIL-CS 1.4 times greater than that adsorbed on parent SBA-15 due to the incorporation of chitosan, which provided a large number of adsorption sites for the laccase attachment (Kadam et al., 2018). In contrast to SBA-Lac, the activity of SBA-CIL-CS-Lac was considerably increased $(75.3\%)$ that might be attributed to the fact that the inorganic mesoporous material SBA-15 combined with chitosan biopolymer using carboxylated IL as bridging molecule could efficiently retain the secondary and tertiary structure of laccase during immobilization and, hence, preserve the biologically active conformation of laccase and prevent the loss of activity (Xiang et al., 2018).
Generally, the use of cross-linking molecules during enzyme immobilization enhances the binding of biomolecules to support carriers. However, conventional crosslinkers react aggressively with enzymes, causing a substantial loss of their biological activities (Sheldon, and van Pelt, 2013). Kumar and coworkers, (2014) immobilized laccase onto amino-coated MNPs using glutaraldehyde as a cross-linking agent and found that the MNPs-immobilized biocatalyst achieved elevated enzyme loading, outstanding reusability, and stability, but the recovered activity of immobilized laccase was very low (about $32\%$ ). In this juncture, dialdehyde starch having multiple active aldehydes and hydroxyl groups not only exhibit considerable biocompatibility like starch but also serves as a coupling molecule for effective enzyme attachment (Sojitra et al., 2017). This long-chain polymer provides greater conformational amendment, chain flexibility, and electrostatic repulsion, which prevents agglomeration, diminish the diffusional limitations, and augments the biocatalytic potentiality of encapsulated enzymes (Tang et al., 2003). Yang et al. (2019) claimed that In contrast to glutaraldehyde, dialdehyde starch mediated immobilization of enzyme onto MNPs showed superior reusability and elevated storage stability. Qiu et al. $(20\mathbf{\bar{1}}.\mathbf{\bar{\mu}})$ fabricated a promising biocatalytic system by immobilizing laccase enzyme $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\mathbf{\lambda}_{\mathrm{~}}^{\bullet}$ amino-functionalized ILs modified MNPs using dialdehyde starch as a cross-linking molecule (Figure 8). The resultant Fe3O4-NILDAS@lac showed good activity retention $\mathrm{(>70\%)}$ , immobilization yield efficiency $(>$ $85\%$ ), and $>80\%$ of its $\mathsf{r}_{\mathbf{l}_{i}}$ ginal activity after storage for 30 days. It functioned in a broader $\mathrm{\pH},$ and temperature perturbations and maintained high stability $(>80\%$ of activity) after six repeated reaction batches.
## 5. Metal-organic frameworks for laccase immobilization
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are an intriguing family of porous nanostructures that have received extraordinary interest as novel immobilization support owing to their high surface area, tunable porosity, high steadiness, larger pore volume, and mild fabrication environments (Wang et al., 2019a). These fascinating features appear as important parameters for exploiting as proficient catalytic carrier support, which promotes mass transfer and provides higher thermal and chemical protection. Many researchers have explored the use of MOFs as a novel matrix for encapsulating a range of different enzymes such as glucose oxidase, lipase, horseradish peroxidase, cytochrome c, etc. (Somturk et al., 2015; Wang et al., 2017; Zhu et al., 2018). All these studies reported the increased stability and multiple reuses of the immobilized enzymes due to restricted movement by MOFs and reprocessing via simple centrifugation. Nevertheless, the development of immobilized laccases with improved catalytic performance remains challenging.
Various strategies and tactics can be realized to synthesize $\Delta A C G_{2}^{-}$ -enzyme matrices based on the availability of different types of bonds. Each of these approaches is associated with merits and demerits. Attachment of an enzyme on the surface of MOF is probably the most elegant strategy that has been inspected by either physical adsorption or covalent linking (Mehta et al., 2016). MOFs with the presence of pendant functional groups or those providing post-synthetic modification should be a prime choice for considering as support matrices (Jung et al., 2011). Though simple steps can realize physical adsorption of enzymes on MOFs, the enzyme is likely to be leached out due to relatively weak binding with MOF. To solve this problem, covalent linking is regarded as a good option by chemical modification of the substrate or labeling enzymes with dye molecules (Jung et al., 2011: Shih et al., 2012). The most fascinating perspective of the surface immobilization method is that there is no restriction on the size of the enzyme, and catalytic activity is often improved when the active sites remain exposed to the substrate. Some limitations of this approach are inadequate material stability and lack of sufficient reusability compared to other methods. Also, the chemical steps involved in adhering enzymes to MOF surfaces may be time-consuming and might also generate organic waste. The enzyme diffusion into the pores of pre-fabricated MOFs is the second strategy to constitute MOF-enzyme biocatalytic supports. Trapping of enzymes within the pores together with their functional interactions with the MOF ligands improve the biocatalyst stability, and delivers substantially augmented reusability (Lykourinou et al., 2011). Since the pore dimensions of most MOFs is much lesser than the size of enzymes, the diffusion route has limited applicability. Recently, MOFs with a larger pore size of $9.8\mathrm{nm}$ have been reported, but concerns still persist over the feasibility of the diffusion strategy in the case of many industrially-relevant enzymes (Deng et al., 2012; Feng et al., 2015). The MOF-enzyme bioconjugate can also be prepared by in-situ encapsulation of enzymes within the MOF structure. This route of immobilization could offer maximum stability due to the rigid encapsulation of enzymes. Thus, the enzyme leaching can be minimized to yield a high degree of reusability, leading to reduced processing costs. This biocatalytic strategy has been successfully used to immobilize even those enzymes with sizes larger than the pore dimensions of the MOF. In contrast to the free forms, the catalytic activity might be lowered in some of the cases partially due to the limited substrate diffusion into the micropores of the MOF. Substrates having a larger size than the pore diameter of the MOF are not easily reachable to the encapsulated biocatalyst (Liang et al., 2015). Therefore, the structural properties of both the enzyme and the MOF should be considered before adopting this method.
Zhang et al. (2020), for the first time, demonstrated that combining laccase with cofactor ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions HKUST-1 results in 1.5-fold improved catalytic activity than that to the free form of the enzyme due to the synergistic oxidation of the substrate. The engineered HKUST-1@laccase bio-nano composite performed as promising biocatalyst under unfavorable conditions of high temperature, acidic $\mathrm{\pH},$ and organic solvents. A novel MOF@biocatalyst nanobiocatalytic system was fabricated by the covalent attachment of laccase onto a porous matrix of zeolitic imidazole framework-8 (ZIF-8) and successfully used for the degradation of pollutants (Mahmoodi and Abdi, 2019). Laccase was immobilized onto amino-modified magnetic MOF, Fe3O4-NH2@MIL-101(Cr). The immobilized biocatalyst prepared by covalent binding and adsorption methods showed high immobilization capacity, appreciable activity recovery, and better resistance elevated temperature and extreme $\mathrm{\pH}$ conditions. It also showed marked stability retaining higher than $85\%$ of its original performance after preserving for about one month. After $6\mathrm{~h~}$ incubation at $85\ ^{\circ}C,$ the nanobiocatalyst presented $49.1\%$ remaining activity. The stability was also greatly enhanced in organic solvents such as methanol. Likewise, magnetic ZIF-8 nanoparticles synthesized via an easy approach have demonstrated as a platform for immobilizing recombinant laccase from $E$ . coli (Figure 9). The immobilized biocatalyst achieved high activity recovery $(75.7\%)$ , as well as, improved thermal and storage stability. It maintained over $\pmb{15}\%$ of original activity after incubating at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $6\mathrm{h},$ whereas the form of the enzyme was inactivated under these conditions. In addition to $87.1\%$ activity retention after 10 days of storage, the immobilized laccase was able to reuse for five repeated batches (Wang et al., 2019b). In conclusion, the immobilized laccases onto MOFs offer a cost-effective and facile route for numerous applications.
## 6. Other nanostructured materials for laccase immobilization
Immobilization of laccases on multiple nanoparticles is getting interested as impressive biocatalytic systems in industrial-scale applications. TiO2-based immobilization has become a promising approach owing to semiconductivity, large surface area, better stability, low cost, good biocompatibility, and high coordination capacity with carboxyl groups and amine. TiO2 possesses a simple structure containing a single functional group. It can be easily modified by functional groups to creating robust immobilization support for enzyme immobilization (Uzunoğlu, 2017; Sanguanpak et al., 2017). Several enzymes, including ascorbate oxidase, horseradish peroxidase, laccase, tyrosine, Glucose oxidase, and penicillin acylase have been immobilized on titanium dioxide nanoparticles (Romero-Arcos et al., 2016; Sanguanpak et al., 2017; Wang et al., 2019c). TiO2 surface modification is an effective strategy to improve the biocompatibility between enzymes and carriers, which subsequently magnifies the loading capacity than other immobilization carriers like cellulose, starch, agarose, collagen, and clay (Dedania et al., 2020). A commercial laccase was covalently bonded with the TiO2 nanoparticles sequentially functionalized with 3-aminopropyltriethoxysilane and glutaraldehyde (Hou et al., 2014; Ji et al., 2016). The resulted nanoimmobilized biocatalyst preserved its significant catalytic activity and presented high operative stability. Ji and coworkers, (2017) introduced a novel strategy for direct immobilization of enzyme extracts onto the surface of modified TiO2 nanoparticles. The nanoparticles immobilized biocatalytic system showed the same catalytic potential to the immobilized commercial enzymes. The proficient isolation and immobilization of laccase on $\mathrm{\pm}\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{-}}^{\mathsf{^{\prime}}}$ nanoparticles showed a considerable promise for environmental remediation. Cu2+-chelated $\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ and ZnO nanoscale particles synthesized by green sources were used to attach laccase via the metal-affinity adsorption process. ZnO nanoclusters showed a strong immobilization of laccase followed by MnO2 nanospheres. Enzyme activities of both immobilized systems were 2-times greater than free than that to the free form of laccase. Experimental data revealed ZnO immobilized laccase a superior catalyst than ZnO and free counterpart. Overall, MnO2 and ZnO are found potential platforms for scale-up immobilization of laccases with augmented stability and reuse properties.
Cooperative catalyst systems have recently been devised as sustainable and highly efficient alternatives to traditional counterparts. In this catalyst, two or more catalytic centers are cooperated to minimize the energy requirement for chemical transformation reactions. Many research groups prepared oxidation cooperative systems to execute catalytic reactions under ambient environments (Gigant and Bäckvall, 2014; Mekmouche et al., 2015). Despite the significant advances in these protocols, some drawbacks exist, such as homogeneity of the catalyst systems, and the utilization of expensive ligands and transition metal complexes. In this context, Moradi et al. (2020) developed a novel and recyclable hybrid biocatalytic that consist of palladium nanoparticles along with laccase enzyme with extraordinary reactivity. This versatile cooperative catalyst was designed by sequential coupling of laccase and Pd as a nanometallic element into siliceous mesocellular foams (MCF) cavity. The assynthesized biocatalytic oxidation system demonstrated elevated activity for the aerobic oxidation of alcohols, cyclohexanone, and cyclohexanol as well as aerobic dehydrogenation of N-heterocyclic compounds.
## 7. Applications of nanomaterials-immobilized laccase
## 7.1 Degradation of phenolic compounds
Phenolic compounds are commonly detected in wastewater effluents liberated from a variety of agricultural and industrial sources such as pulp mill and olive oil wastewaters, coal conversion wastes, and production $\cot$ plastics, resins, leather, and adhesives. In contrast to traditional wastewater treatment strategies for phenolic removal including adsorption, photocatalysis, biological process, chemical treatment such as Fenton reaction, and ozonation, enzymatic treatment of these wastewater effluents appeared highly promising owing to high specificity, and negligible ecological impact (Villegas et al., 2016). Costa et al. (2019) employed CNTs/polysulfone membranes immobilized laccases-based system for successful elimination of an aqueous solution containing four different kinds of phenolic compounds, which are often found in various industrial waste namely resorcinol, phenol, 4-chlorophenol and 4-methoxy phenol (Ahmed et al., 2011; Sampaio et al., 2013). The redox potential of laccase $(500-800\mathrm{mV}_{\it\cdot}$ ) and molecules structures of pollutants exert a strong influence on the oxidation capability of enzymes towards these compounds in the solution (Gianfreda et al., 2003). 4-methoxy phenol was easily oxidized by immobilized laccase MP due to the lowest redox potential. On the other hand, phenol and 4-chlorophenol with high redox potentials $(800~\mathrm{{mV}})$ ) were found to be resistant by enzyme treatment (Johannes and Majcherczyk, 2000). The laccase-containing MWCNTs-modified electrospun fibrous membranes efficiently catalyzed the removal of bisphenol A from the aqueous system at $\mathrm{pH}~7.0$ and $25\pm1{}^{\circ}{\mathrm{C}}$ . Notably, the removal performance was recorded to be over $90\%$ when $2\mathrm{mg}$ of laccase was added into $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of BPA solution $(10\mathrm{mg/L})$ and incubated with shaking for $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ (Dai et al., 2016b). Immobilization of laccase from C. unicolor on porous silica beads excluded about $90\%$ of bisphenol A within $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ (Songulashvili et al., 2012). Laccase attached to Fe3O4-NIL-DAS excellently transformed the phenolic pollutants such as phenol, 4-chlorophenol, and 2,4- dichlorophenol in a broader $\mathrm{\pH}$ and temperature ranges. It showed $93.6\%$ , $86.1\%,$ , and $100\%$ efficacies for the removal of 4-chlorophenol, phenol, and 2,4-dichlorophenol, respectively, and retained over $80\%$ of its primary catalytic activity after six repeated degradation cycles (Qiu et al., 2019b). Wu et al. (2019) assessed the application of laccase immobilized on amino-modified MOF, $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}-N H_{2}}@_{\perp\sqrt{\perp}}^{\star\star\star}\mathsf{\Pi}_{-1}01(\mathrm{Cr})$ to eliminate 2,4- dichlorophenol from the aqueous solution. MOF-coupled laccase $(3.0~\mathrm{g/L})$ showed a rapid removal of 2,4-dichlorophenol $(10\mathrm{mg/L})$ in $\therefore\frac{1}{2}$ first hour and eventually reached up to $87\%$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The adsorption by the $\mathrm{Fe^{\prime}J\cdot{N H_{2}}@M I L\cdot101(C r)}$ contributed about $58\%$ of the 2,4-dichlorophenol removal, while the rest of the degradation occurred by the catalytic action of laccase. After the reaction completion, the nanobiocatalyst was easily recovered from the mixture and reused in multiple cycles of wastewater treatment (Figure 10). Based on all the research findings, it can be inferred that the immobilization of laccase onto nanomaterials is an efficient and novel approach to degrade recalcitrant phenolic compounds.
## 7.2 Degradation of pharmaceutical compounds and xenobiotics
Pharmaceutical compounds constitute a large number of commonly recommended therapeutic medicines/drugs (Carballa et al., 2005; Sill and von Recum, 2008). However, the discharge of active residues of pharmaceuticals in the aquatic system generates substantial concerns due to their potential adverse consequences on human health and other environmental compartments (Cunningham et al., 2009). Diclofenac is among the important nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug, which is widely identified in water because it is routinely prescribed to treat acute muscle pain, arthritis, and ankylosing spondylitis (Vieno and Sillanpää, 2014). The broad-spectrum global consumption of this drug results in contaminating ground and surface water. It disrupts the normal biochemical and metabolic functions of fish and eventually leads to tissue damage even at very low concentrations (Saravanan et al., 2011). Furthermore, diclofenac pollution has caused huge mortalities in vulture species (Naidoo and Swan, 2009). Consequently, its removal from the environmental milieu is indispensable before it becomes part of the food chain. The degradation of pharmaceutical compounds and xenobiotics by nanomaterial-immobilized laccase has been reported in many recent studies. For example, Xu et al. (2015) reported a higher diclofenac removal efficiency by the PVA/CS/MWNTs membrane immobilized laccase compared to laccase-PVA/CS membrane. Complete degradation of diclofenac ( $\underline{{?}}.5\mathrm{mg/L})$ was achieved by the catalytic action of laccase-PVA/CS/MWNTs membrane, whereas the laccase-PVA/CS without MWCNTs showed $84.9\%$ removal after 6 h of reaction time. GO-immobilized laccase has shown a considerable capability $^{\iota}{_{0}}$ degrade diclofenac and ibuprofen. GCMS analysis identified 4-Isobutylacetophenone as a degradation product of ibuprofen, while diclofenac was degraded into and 2,6-Dichloroaniline, N- (2,6-Dichlorophenyl) indolin-2-one, and 2-(2,6-Dichloro-phenylamino) benzaldehyde by the laccase-catalyzed reaction (Yusuf, 2020). Recently, Maryškov{ et al. (2020) evaluated the potential of polyamide/polyethyleneimine nanofibers coupled laccase for the degradation of a concentrated solution of endocrine-disrupting compounds $(10\ \mathrm{\mg/L}$ of each contaminant). The immobilized laccase-based nanobiocatalytic system presented enhanced removal efficiency resulting in $17.5\%$ , $73.6\%$ , $27.9\%$ , and $47.3\%$ elimination of diclofenac, triclosan, bisphenol A, and $17\alpha$ -ethinylestradiol, respectively when incubated for $20\mathrm{h}$ at room temperature under mild agitation $(80~\mathrm{rpm})$ . García-Morales and coworkers (2018) carried out the removal of a mixture of micropollutants including diclofenac and acetaminophen by the use of immobilized laccase $(100~\mathrm{U/L})$ . The maximum biotransformation of diclofenac was $68\%$ after $8\mathrm{h},$ , and over $90\%$ biotransformation of acetaminophen was reached after $2\mathrm{h}$ of treatment at pH 4. Jia et al.
(2019) demonstrated that Lac-MIL-53(Al) was able to complete removal triclosan in $2\mathrm{h},$ which is attributed to the synergistic contribution of the meso-MIL-53(Al) adsorption and biocatalytic degradation of immobilized laccase. It also preserved about $60\%$ of its preliminary activity in eight consecutive reuse cycles without any desorption. LC/MS/MS and GC/MS characterization revealed the transformation of triclosan to 2, 4- DCP by laccase catalyzed treatment through the cleavage of the ether linkage, or oxidation of phenoxyl radicals. Figure 11 represents possible laccase-mediated transformation pathways of triclosan according to the identified intermediates and products. The phenoxyl radicals were combined via ortho-para, ortho-ortho, and parapara linking among C–O, and C–C bonds resulting in the formation of dimers, which were further attached with phenoxyl radicals giving rise to trimers, or tetramers via self-coupling.
## 7.3 Degradation of dyes— treatment of wastewaters
Several industrial processes consume a large number of dyes for various purposes. It is estimated that over 700,000 tons of different dyes are manufactured every year (Mahmoodi, 2013). Many of these dyes generate toxic metabolites and intermediates during the process of degradation. If not appropriately subjected to treatment, the release of industrial effluents will release massive amounts of dyes into water streams. Therefore, it is imperative to develop a novel technique and materials for the effective treatment of colored wastewater. In comparison to physicochemical techniques, enzyme-mediated biodegradation processes have been conceived promising because of excellent catalytic performance, environmentally friendliness, and appreciable recycling efficiency. Carrier-supported laccases have aroused enormous scientific interest for dyes remediation due to high efficiency and non-toxicity (Cannatelli and Ragauskas, 2017). Immobilized laccase on nanoparticles of zeolite and GO-zeolite nanocomposites showed enhanced efficiency for the degradation of an organic dye Direct Red 23 (Mahmoodi and Saffar-Dastgerdi, 2020). The biotransformation of two anionic dye pollutants including Acid Blue 92 and Direct Red 23 was observed to be more than $75\%$ after six repeated cycles by a covalently linked laccase onto GO nanosheets (Kashefi et al., 2018). Chen et al. (2017) achieved 91.4, 94.7, and $95.6\%$ decolorization of brilliant green, crystal violet, and malachite green dyes $(50\mathrm{mg/L})$ , respectively, by magnetic GOconjugated laccase ( $\mathrm{10~mg}$ for $3.0\ \mathrm{mL}$ of dye solution) at $35~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ after $180\ \mathrm{min}$ of incubation. In the presence of free and MIL-100(Fe) immobilized ABTS, the laccase treatment achieved $95\%$ and $94\%$ decolorization yields for indigo carmine dye, respectively. The tested decolorization conditions were $50\approx\mathrm{g}_{I}^{\prime\prime}.$ dye, $2000\ \mathrm{U/L}$ laccase, and either the free mediator $25~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ for ABTS at $\mathrm{pH}\ \overset{-}{\underset{\rightharpoondown}{\scriptscriptstyle\cdot\mho}}\pi$ the immobilized mediator $(0.25~\mathrm{g/L}$ for ABTS@MIL-100(Fe) at $\mathrm{pH}5.5\rangle$ ). The experiments were conducted in $5~\mathrm{mL}$ centrifuge tubes, and the mixtures were allowed to react at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 150 rpm for 50 min (Liu et al., 2017). The decolorization of dye occurred through the cleavage of the chromophoric group by as-synthesized lac/ABTS@MIL-100(Fe) system, and consequently, a possible biocatalytic mechanism has been suggested (Figure 12). In this pathway, MIL-100(Fe) immobilized ABTS mediator is first transformed into the ABTS+ radical cation through enzyme catalytic reaction with the concomitant reduction of molecular oxygen to water. The participation of the resulting ABTS+ radical in nonenzymatic reactions with the dye molecules reduces ABTS+ to ABTS and oxidizes dye to accomplish a single catalytic cycle. In this way, the dye molecules are eventually completely degraded (Liu et al., 2017). Gahlout et al. (2017) applied laccase-coupled nanoparticles for the degradation of Reactive Violet 1 dye and achieved more than $95\%$ decolorization efficiency at optimal $\mathrm{pH}~5.0$ and $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ after $^{12\mathrm{~h,~}}$ and the resulted metabolites were found less toxic than the parent dye molecules. The analysis revealed two oxidative cleavage regions, and as a result, a degradation pathway was proposed (Figure 13).
## 7.4 Biosensing
Fabrication of biosensors is of supreme interest owing to rapid, economical, accurate, selective, and sensitive detection of target analytes from the samples. Electrochemical biosensors are generally dependent on the generation and consumption of electrons by enzyme-mediated catalysis. Laccase-aided biosensors have shown fascinating applications in the sensing and recognition of phenolic substances in the wastewaters and food industry. They have also found uses in bioremediation and biomedical applications. To prevent the loss of enzyme, it is necessary to securely attach an enzyme with the electrode and should be spread over the transducer elements for quick recognition (Fernández-Fernández et al., 2013). Lou et al. (2020) constructed novel biosensors by immobilizing laccase on to polyaniline/magnetic graphene (PANI/MG) nanocomposites. The resultant nanomaterials-immobilized laccase grafted electrode showed exceptional electrical features such as substantial sensitivity, linear range (0.4– $337.2~\mathrm{\upmumol/L})$ , and limit of detection $(\underset{\cdots}{\overset{\cdot}{\lrcorner}}4\underset{\underset{\cdot}{\star}}{\cdot}\mathsf{M})$ to detect hydroquinone. Nazari et al. (2019) designed a new biosensor for the reliable detection of catechol in real samples. For this, a glassy carbon electrode was coated via GO nanoassemblies and amended with a gold-cetyltrimethylammonium bromide nanocomposite for the adsorptive immobilization of laccase on the surface of the electrode. Immobilized laccase onto the renewed glassy carbon electrode exhibited a direct electron exchange reaction between the electrode and laccase enzyme. A 3D-GN/Cu/Fe3O4 biosensor synthesized by modifying the electrode with laccase presented a high sensitivity for the determination of bisphenol A. It also exhibits a wider linear range, and outstanding stability (Lou et al., 2019). The GC/rGO/Lac biosensor has been efficiently employed for dopamine determination in plasmatic serum and synthetic urine samples, reaching a limit of detection of 91.0 nmol $\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ (Kohori et al., 2018). Pang and coworkers, (2010) investigated the electrocatalytic response of 1-AP functionalized CNTs-immobilized laccase for oxygen reduction using ABTS. The resulted Lac/AP-CNTs nanocatalyst presented high stability and better electrocatalytic activities compared to the enzyme attached to pristine CNTs. More recently, Othman and Wollenberger, (2020) fabricated a new biosensing device for phenolic determination by encapsulating a chemically modified laccase onto screen-printed carbon electrodes (SPCEs) (Fig. 14). The electrochemical properties of COOH–MWCNT-coated SPCEs immobilized laccase were optimal when laccase was subjected to amination before adsorbing in the working electrode. The asfabricated biosensor showed high sensitivity and good response towards the detection and sending of several phenolic compounds. The maximal response was achieved for ABTS $\scriptstyle\prime I_{\mathrm{max}}=27.94\ \upmu\mathrm{A},$ . Likewise, the highest electro-catalytic efficiency was obtained for ABTS $(5588~\upmu\mathrm{A}~\upmu\mathrm{M}^{-1})$ and then by syringaldazine $15\hat{\mathrm{v}}^{1}4~\upmu\mathrm{A}.\upmu\mathrm{M}-1\right)$ . The developed biosensor was substantially stable retaining $99.5\%$ , $8\dot{2}\%,$ and $77\%$ of response after 4, 8, and 10 consecutive reuse cycles using catechol as a catalytic substrate. It maintained activity even after 20 days of storing.
## 7.5 Lignin degradation and valorization
In the wake of depleting fossil fuel resources, there is an unprecedented concern to meet the increasing world’s demand for fuels. In addition, the rampant population growth, and “food versus fuel” discussion, have diverted the emphasis on the synthesis of biofuel from non-edible feedstock’s (Nanthagopal et al., 2017; Sani et al., 2018). Biofuels are eco-sustainable and possess high-energy proficiency than that to fossil-based fuels. The depolymerization and elimination of lignin from lignocellulosic biomass is a requisite step before transforming into biofuels by fermentation technology (Asadi and Zilouei, 2017). In contrast to chemical and physical treatments, ligninolytic enzymesmediated lignin degradation is a green approach that presents numerous benefits such as low energy input, mild operational conditions, and circumventing the use of hazardous chemicals (Sherpa et al., 2018). Shanmugam et al. (2020) immobilized laccase from Trichoderma asperellum onto Fe3O4@SiO2-chitosan nanocarrier support for lignocellulosic biomass delignification and then exploited for biohydrogen synthesis. As compared to the free enzyme, the immobilized biocatalyst showed high potential for lignin removal in sweet sorghum Stover and recycled in eight repeated cycles. The biohydrogen production rate and yields were recorded to be 25 L H2/L-d, 2.8 mol $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{mol}$ reducing sugar, respectively, in a continuous stirred fermenter. In another study, laccase immobilized onto MNPs exhibited greater removal of three lignin model compounds, phethethoxybenzene, 2-phenoxy-1-phenyl ethanol, and benzyl phenyl ether ( $\mathrm{1{mg}}$ substrate) as compared to the free enzyme (Chen et al., 2020). Immobilization enhanced the biodegradation efficacy of laccase towards 2-phenoxy-1- phenyl ethanol and reduced the substrate content in the product from $75.75\%$ to $37.89\%$ . In contrast to the free enzyme, the catalytic products of MnPs-immobilized laccase have a 6-fold increase in 2-styrylphenol, 4-fold in phenol, and 2-phenyloxirane, and 1.5-fold enhancement in other products. It reduced the benzyl phenyl ether content from $95.90\%$ to $75.63\%$ after $48\mathrm{h}$ agitation in a shaker at $25^{\circ}C_{\cdot}$ Muthuvelu et al. (2019) demonstrated the laccase immobilization on ferrite magnetic nanoparticles and copper ferrite magnetic nanoparticles and applied to lignin removal. For this, the Ipomoea carnea residue $(15\mathrm{g})$ were used in shake flasks $(250\mathrm{mL})$ ) along with free laccase $(0.5\%$ $\mathbf{v}/\mathbf{v})$ and immobilized forms $(\mathrm{\Delta\langleoc\mathfrak{m}g\rangle}$ in sodium citrate buffer $(50\mathrm{mM},\ \mathrm{pH}\ 4.8)$ , and incubated at $30\pm2{\ }^{\circ}{\mathrm{C}}$ and 120 rpm for $20\mathrm{h}$ . Results revealed that the CuMNPsimmobilized laccase presented high delignification efficiency $(43.28\pm1.46\%$ , $160.6\mathrm{mg}$ lignin/g biomass) followed by MNPs-coupled laccase $(40.10\pm1.35\%$ , 169.5 mg lignin/g biomass) and free laccase $38.16\pm0.78\%$ 175.5 mg lignin/g biomass). In conclusion, the immobilized laccase could be effectively used for lignin valorization in multiple cycles.
## 8. Conclusions and future perspectives
With key scientific advances in the bio-catalysis or protein engineering, in particular, the progress of highly robust and multifunctional biocatalysts is uninterruptedly intensifying with applied perspectives. In recent years, the development of new nanostructured carriers, as support matrices, for enzyme immobilization has enabled to engineer robust biocatalytic cues. Owing to the unique physicochemical, structural and functional attributes of nano-engineered matrices, such as high surface-to-volume ratio, surface area, potent chemical and mechanical stability, surface pendant functional groups, outstanding optical, thermal, and electrical characteristics, all make them robust platform matrices for enzyme immobilization, as compared to the immobilization on planar two-dimensional (2-D) surface materials. Hence, the exploitation and deployment of nanostructured-assisted immobilized enzymes have been a subject of pronounced interest, in current years, to flourish the nano-biocatalysts-mediated industrial biotransformation and bioprocesses. Some materials like graphene and its derivatives, carbon nanotubes, and nanoparticles firmly stimulate the mechanical characteristics, such as stiffness and elasticity of the support material, and offer biocompatible environments for enzyme immobilization. Another notable merit of using nanostructured-assisted immobilized enzymes is that they avoid undesirable and sometimes uncontrolled side reactions catalyzed by contaminant residues of crude enzyme-based preparations. In addition, nanostructured-assisted immobilized enzymes can significantly induce the overall reaction efficacy, durability, and robustness of the catalytic cues for their sustainable reusability/recyclability, which, in turn, diminish or at least lower the overall cost of the bio-catalytic processes, from the industrial perspective. In this $\cos^{n+}\exp_{-}^{2}$ even increasing scientific awareness and fundamental understanding of the physiochemical and surface available pendant functional attributes nanostructured carriers and catalytic characteristics of engineered biocatalysts are significantly indispensable to develop sustainable, innovative bioprocesses for multipurpose applications, such as the degradation of emergent contaminants, biosensing cues, lignin deconstruction, and high-value products of industrial interests. In conclusion, the continuous progress in enzyme engineering is a unique way that offers robust bio-catalytic tools to strengthen the research for industrial bioprocesses. From the future perspectives, the integration of various in-practice classical enzyme immobilization approaches and nanostructured-assisted bio-catalysis/protein engineering strategies could be the most efficient for generating a powerful, recyclable, and catalytically robust bio-catalysts that fit best for the real-world biotechnological processes.
